{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CgcMXErppqwqNpbJA9JrX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# How a Bicycle Rim Brake Works

When the rider squeezes the brake lever, they pull a cable that runs from the handlebar down to the brake mechanism. This cable is under tension and transmits the pulling force directly to the brake caliper, which is mounted near the wheel.

The brake caliper uses this cable tension to push two rubber brake pads against the sides of the wheel rim. As the pads press firmly against the rim's surface, friction is created between the rubber and the metal. This friction slows the wheel's rotation, bringing the bicycle to a stop or reducing its speed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":64,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":131,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}

