Primary succession describes the establishment of a new community of organisms in an area that has never been colonized before, such as after a volcanic eruption. The lava creates bare rock with no soil or existing organisms, so the process begins from scratch with pioneer species (like lichens and mosses) gradually building up soil and allowing other organisms to establish themselves.

